I have been puzzling about similar symptoms, too.  So has one of
my friends, and he uses X86 hardware.

My setup: I have a UP2000, a Yamaha 8424 burner, and a Plextor
wide reader.  Both the burner and reader are fed from the
single-ended motherboard SCSI connector, with a high quality
terminator on the end of the cable and all drive termination
turned off.  I don't have access to non-Alpha hardware to do
comparisons.  I use cdrecord to burn audio and data.  I use
cdparanoia to read audio discs back on the Plextor reader to
verify them.  (I have a custom C program to skip past the padding
cdrecord puts on the CD.)

My symptoms: Up until a few months ago, I had virtually no
problems with burning either audio or data CDs.  Then, a few
months ago (maybe as much as six months ago), I started getting
considerable amounts of both '-' and '+' characters in
cdparanoia's progress bars, sometimes an incorrect extraction,
and occasionally cdparanoia would bomb out.  Discs I burn have
always played successfully on a 10-year-old Pioneer PDM-630 and
the CD player in a 1999 Suburban (factory Delco player).

My hunches: I had suspected either a bad batch of blank discs
(TDK) or a problem with either the burner or reader (more
suspicious of the Plextor reader).  Maybe blank discs, so much
less expensive at retail than they were a year or two ago, are
just being manufactured with looser quality control.

My friend's hunches: My friend, who does not use any Alpha
hardware, has been noticing similar symptoms of greatly dimishing
reliability over a time period of a year or so.  He suspected
more recent burners and/or readers had watermarking hardware or
firmware, and the drives were getting false positive detection of
watermarks.

Unfortunately, I'm not of much worth in providing answers.  If
anybody else does have answers, I'm eager to hear about them,
though.

