Hi Alpha Fans,

I have been experiencing some really bizarre problems which seem to affect 
cd recording ONLY on my Alpha.

Here's the scenario.  I am trying to burn some audio CDs, but the resulting 
discs wouldn't play properly on some CD players and CDROM drives.  In the 
course of hours of experimenting I have tried:

3 different burners (2 IDE and 1 SCSI)
4 different makes of media
several different versions of cdrecord (including cdrecord-1.11-0.a40), 
cdrdao and Nero
3 computers (my LX164 Alpha, a PPro running mandrake, a Pentium running 
Win2000)
kernel 2.4.19 and 2.4.20

I cannot claim to have tried every single permutation of the above, but one 
simple fact shines through the results; vis It always works fine on the 
other machines and always produces problems on the Alpha.

To be specific.  When the media burned on the Alpha is played in some CD 
players and some older CDROM drives the player usually recognizes the 
layout of the CD and sometimes plays the first track OK, but it won't skip 
to other tracks or fast forward and generally acts very unreliably.

So, ideas?  cdrecord doesn't generate any errors, and gives every indication 
of having worked just fine.  My alpha used to be able to burn CDs perfectly 
a year or so ago when I was running an older kernel and an older cdrecord 
and associated libraries and possibly before I switched from using MILO to 
SRM and aboot.  I don't really want to have to reverse all these changes to 
find out which one caused the problem.

So, has anyone else seen problems like this?  Remember, the CDs play 
perfectly on some CD players (even old ones), so that fact that you haven't  
seen the problem doesn't prove anything.

Can anyone conceive of what might cause this?  It seems to me that unless 
the sequence of bytes sent to the recorder is perfect the odds of it 
producing anything which works at all is low, so this is clearly a very 
subtle problem.

Ideas?

Thanks,

        Richard Simpson


