William K. Allard <wka@math.duke.edu> wrote:
> I added what I
> believe is an appropriate ks.cfg to generic.img and at the SRM prompt
> entered
> 
>>>> boot dva0

This likely means that you were lucky and 'boot_osflags' variable
was not defined to provide other options so it defaulted to 0.
Unless you _know_ that this what you really want it is safer
to do

    boot dva0 -fl 0

> The boot process started, asked me to insert ramkdisk.img and after a
> few minutes gave me the interactive boot interface indicating that my
> ks.cfg was never read.  I then, in desperation, tried
> 
>>>> boot dva0 ks=floppy
> 
> because I saw something like that somewhere.  It promptly complained
> that ks=floppy was not an allowable option.

Indeed it is not.  You are talking to SRM.  You would have to put
all boot options into

         -fl "..... ks=floppy".

Instead you can boot with 'b dva0 -fl i', list available predefined boot
commands and at 'aboot> ' prompt, and retype what you want adding to it
"ks=floppy".  Easier and with less chances for typing mistakes would be 
to mount that floppy somewhere else, fire up some editor, and add a new
line to 'aboot.cfg' there with whatever options you desire.  Assuming
that it end up with a label "5" then you would boot with 'b dva0 -fl 5'.
 
> I suspect I may be able to edit the aboot.conf file to run kickstart
> but do not know the syntax of this file and am unable to find any
> documentation on it.

At "docs" CD, at least from 7.2 set, there is a well commented
file 'RH-DOCS/sample.ks'.  Also first two chapters of "Customization
Guide" (on the same CD as 'rlh-cg' in few different formats) are
devoted entirely to kickstart issues.

In general you likely will be better of with 7.2 set instead of 7.1.
All in all "docs" disk takes around 594M.  Even taking into account
that the same information is repeated there in multiple formats, and
some of it does not really concerns Alpha, there are few pages to read.

  Michal



