> I'm having some trouble with fast IDE disks in my Alpha machines. They
> work fine, except they won't go above UDMA3/ATA33.  If I set the mode to
> UDMA4, I get a few CRC errors and the driver moves back to UDMA3.  This
> happens using a Promise pdc20268/ultra100 tx2 controller with a Seagate
> and a Western Digital disk.  I even tried a special shielded cable, but
> it didn't help much.  The same thing happens in a Miata and a 164SX.
> Does anyone else have any experience with IDE in Alphas?  Is it simply
> too much noise in the system?

I have a Seagate and a WD hooked up on a Promise Ultra 66 in my 164SX.
Both disks operate at udma4 24/7 without any problem. This configuration
has been in use for the last 2-3 years. I used to get CRC errors (even
kernel oopses) when I had a CD-Rom connected to this controller -- Promise
controllers are known to have problems with cd-roms.

The controller and drives are autotuned to udma4 during boot-up. Tweaking
the controller after that may be very dangerous in the case of Promise
cards; simply trying to set it to a different speed may even lock-up your
computer. The issue with these controllers is that, since they are set to
a specific DMA mode, they can't go back to a lower setting without a
hardware reset. How do you "set the mode to UDMA4"? Are you sure that the
method you follow doesn't try to first reset the controller to, say, PIO4
and then to set it to the desired speed? This is for example what the
linux kernel does...

Bye,
Nick.

